arXiv:2603.11107v3 [math.OC] 27 Oct 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:
<br class="ltx_break">Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit Square
<br class="ltx_break">Using Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nathan Sudermann-Merx
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Institute for Computer Science, DHBW Mannheim. Email: <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nathan.sudermann-merx@dhbw.de</span></span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">October 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) approach for the Heilbronn triangle problem on the unit square.
The formulation exploits the boundary structure of optimal configurations, which we establish rigorously, to fix coordinates and orientation variables a priori.
With this formulation, a general-purpose global solver solves the case <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> to global optimality within its numerical tolerances in about eight minutes on a laptop and without any precomputation, compared with about 23 hours of total computation for the best previous approach.
The same model also solves <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> for the first time, in under seven hours, and confirms the configuration of Comellas and Yebra (2002).
The numerical solutions then guide an exact symbolic reconstruction, and the minimum triangle area of every reconstructed configuration is verified in exact arithmetic.
This yields exact coordinates for all <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, including the configurations of Comellas and Yebra (2002) for <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, together with rigorous lower bounds.
Finally, we derive structural properties of optimal configurations, formulate a conjecture on the number of critical triangles, and pose an open question.
All code and data are publicly available.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>The problem</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Heilbronn triangle problem arises in discrete geometry and asks:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S1.SS1.p2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_center">
<span id="S1.SS1.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.SS1.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How should <math id="S1.SS1.p2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points be placed in the unit square so that the smallest triangle determined by any three of them is as large as possible?</em></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The problem is named after the mathematician Hans Heilbronn (and not after the city in southern Germany). Born into a German-Jewish family, he was dismissed from his position at the University of Göttingen in 1933 and emigrated to England <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. The problem first appeared in print in Roth’s 1951 paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, where the unit square was replaced by an arbitrary closed convex region in the plane of positive measure, and the emphasis was placed on the asymptotic behavior of the minimal triangle area rather than on explicit constructions for small values of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Upper bounds</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the minimal triangle area in an optimal configuration of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points in the unit square by <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Heilbronn conjectured that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> should satisfy an asymptotic upper bound of the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}=O\!\left(\frac{1}{n^{2}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}=O\!\left(\frac{1}{n^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As we will see in Section <a href="#S1.SS3" title="1.3 Lower bounds ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, this turned out to be incorrect.
Roth observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> that the problem admits a simple upper bound,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}\leq\frac{1}{n-2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}\leq\frac{1}{n-2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">To see this, note that the convex hull of any configuration of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points can be partitioned into <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> triangles with pairwise disjoint interiors and total area at most <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that at least one of these triangles must have area no greater than <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Over the following decades, the upper bounds were steadily refined. In 1972, Schmidt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> obtained a significant improvement, which was sharpened by Roth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> later that year and further strengthened by Komlós, Pintz, and Szemerédi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> in 1981, establishing what remained the best-known estimate for over forty years.
A major breakthrough came in 2023, when Cohen, Pohoata, and Zakharov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> proved that for all sufficiently large <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta_{n}\leq n^{-8/7-1/2000}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}\leq n^{-8/7-1/2000},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which is currently the strongest known asymptotic upper bound. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1.2 Upper bounds ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates that this bound provides a reasonably tight envelope for all best-known configurations with <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
