height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="136.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F4.pic1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#005353;">Product Substitution</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B35900;" stroke="#B35900" stroke-width="0.7pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 246.78 21.24 L -3.67 21.24 C -5.2 21.24 -6.43 20 -6.43 18.47 L -6.43 -31.34 C -6.43 -32.87 -5.2 -34.11 -3.67 -34.11 L 246.78 -34.11 C 248.31 -34.11 249.55 -32.87 249.55 -31.34 L 249.55 18.47 C 249.55 20 248.31 21.24 246.78 21.24 Z M -6.43 -34.11"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B35900;--ltx-fill-color:#B35900;" fill="#B35900" stroke="#B35900" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 84.79 26.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0.59em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10.65pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="73.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F4.pic1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#B35900;">Sign Fixing</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B30000;" stroke="#B30000" stroke-width="1.1pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 247.06 83.78 L -3.39 83.78 C -4.92 83.78 -6.16 82.54 -6.16 81.02 L -6.16 -31.06 C -6.16 -32.59 -4.92 -33.83 -3.39 -33.83 L 247.06 -33.83 C 248.59 -33.83 249.83 -32.59 249.83 -31.06 L 249.83 81.02 C 249.83 82.54 248.59 83.78 247.06 83.78 Z M -6.16 -33.83"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B30000;--ltx-fill-color:#B30000;" fill="#B30000" stroke="#B30000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 56.99 89.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.59em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10.65pt;" height="11.07" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="129.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F4.pic1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#B30000;">Symmetry Breaking</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#595959;" stroke="#595959" stroke-width="0.7pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 246.78 104.26 L -3.67 104.26 C -5.2 104.26 -6.43 103.02 -6.43 101.49 L -6.43 -7.82 C -6.43 -9.35 -5.2 -10.59 -3.67 -10.59 L 246.78 -10.59 C 248.31 -10.59 249.55 -9.35 249.55 -7.82 L 249.55 101.49 C 249.55 103.02 248.31 104.26 246.78 104.26 Z M -6.43 -10.59"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#595959;--ltx-fill-color:#595959;" fill="#595959" stroke="#595959" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 64.29 107.1)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.59em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10.65pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="115.77"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F4.pic1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#595959;">Variable Domains</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The final MINLP formulation <math id="S3.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Delta}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Delta}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Colored boxes indicate the core model <math id="S3.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Delta}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Delta}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (blue) and the enhancements: product substitution (teal), sign fixing (orange), symmetry breaking (red), and variable domains (gray).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Variants for the types II and III</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest of the optimal values of three restricted problems. Besides <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Delta}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Delta}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for type I, we therefore solve two variants that differ from <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Delta}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Delta}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> only in the fixings.</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Type II:</em> <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=(0,y_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=(0,y_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}=(x_{2},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}=(x_{2},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{3}=C=(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{3}=C=(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{4}=D=(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{4}=D=(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{5}\leq\dots\leq x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{5}\leq\dots\leq x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The points <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> occur in counterclockwise order, so all triangles among them are positively oriented. In addition, <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pm}_{34k}=\tfrac{1}{2}(1-y_{k})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>34</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pm}_{34k}=\tfrac{1}{2}(1-y_{k})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pm}_{14k}=-\tfrac{1}{2}(1-y_{1})x_{k}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pm}_{14k}=-\tfrac{1}{2}(1-y_{1})x_{k}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Type III:</em> <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=A=(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=A=(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}=C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}=C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{3}=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{3}=D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{4}\leq\dots\leq x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{4}\leq\dots\leq x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pm}_{123}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mn>123</mn><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pm}_{123}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pm}_{23k}=\tfrac{1}{2}(1-y_{k})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>23</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pm}_{23k}=\tfrac{1}{2}(1-y_{k})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pm}_{13k}=-\tfrac{1}{2}x_{k}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pm}_{13k}=-\tfrac{1}{2}x_{k}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The corresponding binary variables are fixed as in Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 Sign fixing ‣ 3.2 Enhancements ‣ 3 Mixed-integer nonlinear optimization models ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a>. All other ingredients of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Delta}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Delta}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are kept.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Comparison with Monji, Modir, and Kocuk</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrent work by Monji, Modir, and Kocuk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> was the first to apply general-purpose MINLP solvers to the Heilbronn problem and solved <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> to global optimality within solver tolerances. Our formulation differs in several respects:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute value handling:</span> Monji et al. linearize <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq|A_{t}^{\pm}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq|A_{t}^{\pm}|</annotation></semantics></math> with binary variables and big-<math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> type coefficients. We use the bilinear constraint <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq(2b_{t}-1)A_{t}^{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq(2b_{t}-1)A_{t}^{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>, which avoids such coefficients and is handled by the solver’s spatial branching.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Symmetry breaking:</span> We fix five boundary points on specific edges in counterclockwise order, eliminating five coordinates outright, whereas Monji et al. order the points by their <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinates. The fixed boundary order also enables sign fixing for <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{5}{3}+(n-5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>5</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{5}{3}+(n-5)</annotation></semantics></math> binary variables.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minimal auxiliary variables:</span> We introduce no edge-proximity, strip-assignment, or grid-cell auxiliary binary variables.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No precomputations:</span> The structural information that Monji et al. obtain in about 23 hours of precomputation follows here from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which is proved analytically (Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Comparison of the total effort ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Comparison of the total effort ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> compares the total computational effort of both approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Computational study</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All computations were carried out on an Apple MacBook Pro with an Apple M5 Pro chip (15-core CPU) and 48 GB of RAM.
The models are implemented in Python 3.12 using Gurobi 13.0.3 through its Python interface <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gurobipy</span>. The symbolic computations use SymPy 1.14.0.
The only non-default Gurobi parameter is <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MIPFocus = 2</span>, which shifts the emphasis towards the dual bound.
All other parameters keep their default values. In particular, the relative optimality gap is <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, the absolute gap <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-10}</annotation></semantics></math>, the feasibility and optimality tolerances <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the integrality tolerance <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. Gurobi used all 15 cores of the machine.
No initial solution is provided, except in the warm-start experiments of Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Initialization ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.
Since running times of MINLP solvers vary with the random seed, every instance of the main experiment is solved with five seeds, and we report the median and the range.
The ablation and comparison runs use a single seed and a time limit of four hours. The baseline for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> has a time limit of one hour, and the runs for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> a time limit of 24 hours.
All tables in this section are generated by a script from the solver output. The code, the logs, and the solution files are part of the accompanying repository.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The tolerances determine what the numbers mean.
A configuration returned by Gurobi satisfies the constraints up to the feasibility tolerance, so its objective value can deviate from the minimum triangle area of its coordinates by about <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the reported objective value can even exceed the true optimum. For <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math>, for example, Gurobi reports <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.07237703" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.07237703</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.07237703</annotation></semantics></math>, while the exact optimal value is <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{13}-1)/36\approx 0.07237642" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.07237642</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{13}-1)/36\approx 0.07237642</annotation></semantics></math>.
Coordinates are therefore reliable to about six decimal places, and the dual bound determines the optimal value only up to the relative gap of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and up to floating-point effects. This is why we use the solver output as numerical evidence and as input to the exact computations of Section <a href="#S5" title="5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and not as a proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the results of the final model <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5,\dots,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5,\dots,9</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
