<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Dual bounds and running times of the variants for the types II and III.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, the bounds of types II and III do not exceed the value of type I within the tolerances, so the optimal value is attained by a configuration of type I. For <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> the bounds are clearly smaller, for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> by 4.7% for type II and by 8.4% for type III, and for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> by 10.1% for both.
For <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, all three types attain the value <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/8</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>, type II attains the same value as type I, as expected, since the optimal configuration has both types (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>).
For <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>, the variants for types II and III are solved in at most 69 seconds. For <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, type II takes 1.5 hours and type III five minutes. Their running times are part of the total effort in Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Comparison of the total effort ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Contribution of the model ingredients</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the contribution of each ingredient of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we remove one ingredient at a time and solve the resulting model for <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7,8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7,8,9</annotation></semantics></math>.
The variants are: no boundary pattern (no fixed coordinates and hence no sign fixing, all points ordered by <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate), no sign fixing, no ordering of the interior points, no product substitution, no bound <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq\Delta_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq\Delta_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and binary variables only for triangles whose sign is not fixed (Remark in Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS3" title="3.2.3 Sign fixing ‣ 3.2 Enhancements ‣ 3 Mixed-integer nonlinear optimization models ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.3</span></a>).
In addition, we test the strip capacities described below.
Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Contribution of the model ingredients ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> also contains the baseline <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{0}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{0}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the warm-start runs of Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Initialization ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, and the runs with Gurobi 11.0.1 of Section <a href="#S4.SS6" title="4.6 Comparison of the total effort ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
