<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Running times in seconds of variants of <math id="S4.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> (seed 0). Entries marked with <math id="S4.T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> reached the time limit of four hours (one hour for the baseline).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The ablation uses a single seed. Since the running times of the full model for <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> vary between 393 and 753 seconds over the seeds, only differences beyond this range are meaningful.
The boundary pattern has by far the largest effect. Without it, the model is about 40 times slower for <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> the relative gap is still 17% after four hours.
The ordering of the interior points comes second. Without it, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> takes 3.4 times as long, and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> ends after four hours with a relative gap of 8%.
Without the sign fixing and without the bound <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq\Delta_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq\Delta_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the running time for <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> roughly doubles (774 and 862 seconds), while <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> are unaffected.
The product substitution and the restriction of the binary variables to the triangles whose sign is not fixed have no measurable effect. A likely reason for the former is that Gurobi introduces auxiliary variables for bilinear terms itself.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Strip capacities.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Monji et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, Table 1]</cite> bound the sorted <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinates by the number of points that fit into a horizontal strip of height <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> without creating a triangle of area below a lower bound <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\leq\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\leq\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and they determine these capacities by solving restricted Heilbronn problems.
The capacities also follow without computation.
An affine scaling maps a strip of height <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> onto the unit square and multiplies all areas by <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/t</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math> points with minimum triangle area at least <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> fit into the strip, their images have minimum area at least <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/t</annotation></semantics></math>, which is impossible if <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H/t&gt;\Delta_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/t&gt;\Delta_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence every strip of height <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;H/\Delta_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;H/\Delta_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> along any edge of the square contains at most <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> points of a configuration with value at least <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
With the proven values <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{3},\dots,\Delta_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{3},\dots,\Delta_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> this reproduces all bounds of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, Table 1]</cite> for <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7,8,9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7,8,9</annotation></semantics></math>, and choosing <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> just below <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H/\Delta_{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H/\Delta_{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> gives slightly stronger ones. For <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>, for example, at most two points lie below <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0.1097" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1097</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0.1097</annotation></semantics></math>, compared with <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq 1/10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq 1/10</annotation></semantics></math> for the third point in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
The resulting constraints are valid for every configuration with value at least <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, independently of labeling and symmetry breaking, so they can be added to <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We model them with one binary variable per point, strip, and edge.
The strip capacities do not speed up the model (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Contribution of the model ingredients ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). For <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>, the running time of 578 seconds lies within the range of the seeds of the full model. The boundary pattern and the ordering of the interior points apparently already contain most of the information that the capacities provide.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Initialization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By default, Gurobi starts without an initial solution and finds feasible configurations with its own heuristics. To assess the effect of a good starting point, we also provide the best-known configuration as a MIP start.
The warm start does not shorten the solution process (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Contribution of the model ingredients ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). For <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>, it takes 572 seconds, which lies within the range of the seeds without a warm start. This matches the observation of Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> that Gurobi finds the optimal configuration within seconds and spends almost all of the time on the dual bound.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Comparison of the total effort</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.6 Comparison of the total effort ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares the total computational effort for <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> with that of Monji, Modir, and Kocuk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
For their approach, the total effort consists of the two runs that establish the near-boundary property (318 and 82,359 seconds), the computation of the variable bounds (35 seconds), and the final solve (633 seconds), about 23 hours in total.
For our approach, it consists of the solves for the three types of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The two computations used different hardware and different versions of Gurobi. Their workstation had 96 cores, ours 15. To separate the effect of the solver version from that of the formulation, we also solved our model with Gurobi 11.0.1, the version used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, on our machine.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
