<td id="S4.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Gurobi 11.0.1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T5.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Apple M5 Pro (15 cores)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T5.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="365" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">365</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">365</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T5.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> (type I)</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Total computational effort for <math id="S4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With seed 0, the three types of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> take 486 seconds in total, and with the slowest seed for type I 846 seconds, compared with about 83,300 seconds for Monji et al.
Under Gurobi 11.0.1, the type I model takes 365 seconds, slightly less than with seed 0 under Gurobi 13.0.3 (393 seconds), so the solver version does not explain the difference.
The final solves are of the same order (633 seconds on 96 cores against 393 to 753 seconds on 15 cores). The difference in the total effort stems from the precomputation, which our approach does not need, since the structural information follows from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.7 </span>The case <math id="S4.SS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, we ran <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the variants for types II and III with a time limit of 24 hours each, with the cap <math id="S4.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq 0.0548818" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.0548818</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq 0.0548818</annotation></semantics></math> of Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Bounds and variable substitution ‣ 3.2 Enhancements ‣ 3 Mixed-integer nonlinear optimization models ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>. No previous computation had attempted this case without structural assumptions. All three runs terminated well before the limit with Gurobi’s default optimality tolerance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Type I took 18,039 seconds (5.0 hours) and 446 million nodes. It ended with the objective value <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0465383" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0465383</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0465383</annotation></semantics></math> and the dual bound <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0465430" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0465430</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0465430</annotation></semantics></math>, a relative gap of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0\cdot 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0\cdot 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Types II and III took 5,463 and 292 seconds, with dual bounds <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0418335" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0418335</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0418335</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0418329" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0418329</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0418329</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Configurations with adjacent corners ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). These lie 10.1% below the type I value, so no configuration of these types comes close to the optimum.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Together, the three runs give <math id="S4.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{10}\leq 0.0465430" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.0465430</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{10}\leq 0.0465430</annotation></semantics></math> numerically, after 6.6 hours in total.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The best-known configuration of Comellas and Yebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> has the exact value <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0465374" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0465374</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0465374</annotation></semantics></math> (Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2\cdot 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2\cdot 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> relative below the dual bound, the same order as for <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Main results ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). The second solution in Gurobi’s solution pool coincides with it up to a symmetry of the square, with a deviation below <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> per coordinate. The final incumbent lies within <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> of it, and its reported objective value exceeds the exact value only because it uses the feasibility tolerance: its true minimum triangle area is <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0465365" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0465365</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0465365</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the configuration of Comellas and Yebra is optimal for <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> within solver tolerances, with the same status as <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> plus the dependence on the numerical bound for <math id="S4.SS7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> through the cap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> derives the exact coordinates of this configuration from the final incumbent with the framework of Section <a href="#S5" title="5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and verifies them in exact arithmetic, as for <math id="S4.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives the rigorous lower bound <math id="S4.SS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{10}\geq 0.0465374" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.0465374</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{10}\geq 0.0465374</annotation></semantics></math>, so the numerical upper bound encloses <math id="S4.SS7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> in an interval of relative width <math id="S4.SS7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.2\cdot 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2\cdot 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Monji et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> report for <math id="S4.SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> a lower bound of <math id="S4.SS7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0465383" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0465383</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0465383</annotation></semantics></math> (computed area <math id="S4.SS7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0465369" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0465369</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0465369</annotation></semantics></math>) after about 800 seconds, under two unproven structural assumptions (exactly two points on each edge, and <math id="S4.SS7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{5}\leq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{5}\leq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math> for the sorted <math id="S4.SS7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinates), and no upper bound within one day. They describe the case as open. Our model needs no such assumption, since Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> covers all configurations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>The optimize-then-refine framework</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>The framework</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach proceeds in two stages.
In the first step, we solve the MINLP formulation <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Delta}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Delta}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> from Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The final model ‣ 3 Mixed-integer nonlinear optimization models ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> with Gurobi to global optimality within the solver tolerances.
The solver returns a numerical configuration together with a dual bound that agrees with its objective value up to the relative gap of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both are computed in floating-point arithmetic (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Setup ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).
From the numerical coordinates we identify the <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">critical triangles</em>, that is, the triangles whose area equals the minimum up to the solver tolerance, and record which points lie on which edges of the unit square.
In the second step, we treat these structural observations as an ansatz for deriving exact symbolic coordinates:
setting the areas of the critical triangles equal yields a system of polynomial equations that can be solved in closed form with a computer algebra system.
Finally, we verify the resulting configuration in exact arithmetic: all <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> triangle areas are computed exactly, and we check that none of them is smaller than the claimed minimum and that exactly the expected triangles attain it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The three results of the pipeline have a different mathematical status, and we keep them apart.
The exact verification makes the reconstructed configuration a <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rigorous lower bound</em> on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It does not prove optimality. The structure used in Step 2 is inferred from the numerical solution, so the solution of the polynomial system is exact only for this structure, and a configuration with a different set of critical triangles is not excluded by Step 2.
The global upper bound comes from Step 1 and is numerical, so it is not a mathematical proof <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
For <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, optimality is proven in the literature (Section <a href="#S1.SS4" title="1.4 Results for small-𝑛-configurations ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>), and our computations confirm these results. For <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, the exact configuration matches the solver’s numerical upper bound, but no rigorous proof of optimality is known.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6</span></span><span id="Thmremark6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key practical concern is the identification of critical triangles and boundary points in the first step, since these decisions rest on numerical data subject to solver tolerances.
In all instances considered here, the gaps are unambiguous:
the area of the smallest non-critical triangle exceeds that of the critical triangles by a margin that is several orders of magnitude larger than the solver tolerance, and every point classified as lying on an edge has a coordinate that deviates from the boundary value by less than <math id="Thmremark6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This clear separation makes the structural input to Step 2 unambiguous in all cases considered.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The pipeline is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1 The framework ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="308.02" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 409.93 308.02" width="409.93"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,308.02) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(204.97,0) translate(0,266.93)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000099;--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" stroke="#000099"><path d="M 192.87 40.82 L -192.87 40.82 C -199.4 40.82 -204.69 35.53 -204.69 29.01 L -204.69 -29.01 C -204.69 -35.53 -199.4 -40.82 -192.87 -40.82 L 192.87 -40.82 C 199.4 -40.82 204.69 -35.53 204.69 -29.01 L 204.69 29.01 C 204.69 35.53 199.4 40.82 192.87 40.82 Z M -204.69 -40.82"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -195 22.49)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:30.48em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:3.88em;font-size:10pt;" height="62.27" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="421.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
