<span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">• Exact symbolic solution of the systems</span></span>
</span></span></span>
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-bottom: -1.0pt;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:281.9pt;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.2.3.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">• Exact verification of all triangle areas</span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#009900;--ltx-fill-color:#F0FFF0;" fill="#F0FFF0" stroke="#009900"><path d="M 192.87 -202.3 L -192.87 -202.3 C -199.4 -202.3 -204.69 -207.59 -204.69 -214.12 L -204.69 -254.84 C -204.69 -261.36 -199.4 -266.65 -192.87 -266.65 L 192.87 -266.65 C 199.4 -266.65 204.69 -261.36 204.69 -254.84 L 204.69 -214.12 C 204.69 -207.59 199.4 -202.3 192.87 -202.3 Z M -204.69 -266.65"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -195 -220.64)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:30.48em;--ltx-fo-height:0.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:2.63em;font-size:10pt;" height="44.97" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="421.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:30.48em;">
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.3.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
<span id="S5.F5.pic1.3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Result:<span id="S5.F5.pic1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Exact coordinates with an exactly verified minimum area (rigorous lower bound) that matches the solver’s numerical upper bound</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.9pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -41.1 L 0 -71.33"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -71.33)"><path d="M 6.64 0 C 5.83 0.2 2.24 1.34 0 2.57 L 0 -2.57 C 2.24 -1.34 5.83 -0.2 6.64 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 5.23 -63.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.65em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:8.5pt;" height="9.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="121.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.F5.pic1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">numerical configuration</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.9pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -162.66 L 0 -192.9"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 0 -192.9)"><path d="M 6.64 0 C 5.83 0.2 2.24 1.34 0 2.57 L 0 -2.57 C 2.24 -1.34 5.83 -0.2 6.64 0 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Methodological pipeline of the paper: numerical global optimization (Step 1) and exact analytical refinement with exact verification (Step 2). The result is a rigorous lower bound, while the upper bound remains numerical.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given the formulation of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Mixed-integer nonlinear optimization models ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Step 1 is a routine solver call. Step 2 is more involved.
The equal-area conditions on the critical triangles form a system of polynomial equations whose unknowns are the free coordinate parameters remaining after boundary constraints and coordinate coincidences have been exploited.
The number <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of these parameters ranges from <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>) to <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>), and we employ three solving strategies, tried in succession, and for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> an additional stationarity condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Direct symbolic solve.</em>
For small systems (<math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>, covering <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5,6,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5,6,8</annotation></semantics></math>), SymPy’s symbolic solver produces exact solutions directly, including the detection of the one-parameter family for <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gröbner basis.</em>
A Gröbner basis of a polynomial ideal with respect to a monomial order is a generating set with particularly good division properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For a lexicographic order it is triangular: the last polynomials involve only the last variables, so the system can be solved by back-substitution, much like Gaussian elimination for linear systems.
For <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>, the solution lies in a cubic extension field, and a lexicographic Gröbner basis computation
yields a triangular system from which all coordinates follow by back-substitution through the minimal polynomial <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19f^{3}-27f^{2}+11f-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19f^{3}-27f^{2}+11f-1=0</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Numeric-to-symbolic refinement.</em>
For <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>, neither a direct solve nor a Gröbner basis computation terminates in reasonable time.
Instead, we apply a numeric-to-symbolic approach: from the numerical coordinates we first identify the underlying number field <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{65})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mn>65</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{65})</annotation></semantics></math> by recognizing <math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{65}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>65</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{65}</annotation></semantics></math> in the simplest coordinate, then express all coordinates as elements of this field using SymPy’s <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nsimplify</span> function with the known algebraic extension.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The numeric-to-symbolic step involves a risk:
numerical proximity does not guarantee algebraic correctness, since the coordinates returned by Gurobi are reliable only to about six decimal places (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Setup ‣ 4 Computational study ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).
For instance, for <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> the spurious expression <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{19}{166}+\tfrac{3\sqrt{65}}{166}\approx 0.26017" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>19</mn><mn>166</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>65</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>166</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.26017</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{19}{166}+\tfrac{3\sqrt{65}}{166}\approx 0.26017</annotation></semantics></math>
is numerically indistinguishable from the true value <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{9}{16}-\tfrac{3\sqrt{65}}{80}\approx 0.26017" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>65</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>80</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.26017</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{9}{16}-\tfrac{3\sqrt{65}}{80}\approx 0.26017</annotation></semantics></math>
at solver precision, yet only the latter satisfies the equal-area equations.
We therefore verify every candidate solution symbolically by substituting it into the polynomial system of the critical triangles.
Any coordinate that fails this check is corrected by fixing the validated coordinates and solving the resulting reduced (and much smaller) subsystem exactly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, the equal-area conditions alone do not determine the configuration. With the coordinate coincidences read off from the numerical solution, <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> parameters remain, and the 16 critical areas take only three distinct forms <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. The equations <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}=A_{2}=A_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}=A_{2}=A_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore leave a curve of solutions. Since the configuration maximizes the common area along this curve, we add the Lagrange condition <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(\nabla A_{1},\nabla A_{2},\nabla A_{3})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(\nabla A_{1},\nabla A_{2},\nabla A_{3})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. A lexicographic Gröbner basis of the resulting system, after the removal of degenerate solutions in which a parameter vanishes, contains the polynomial <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2g-1)(12g^{3}-27g^{2}+20g-4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2g-1)(12g^{3}-27g^{2}+20g-4)</annotation></semantics></math> in the coordinate <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> of the point <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{7}=(g,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{7}=(g,g)</annotation></semantics></math>. The root <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1/2</annotation></semantics></math> is spurious, and the other two parameters follow as polynomials in the real root of the cubic factor. This reproduces the configuration of Comellas and Yebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, who also obtained it by an analytical refinement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The final exact verification is reported in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Step 2 takes less than one second for every <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, and the exact verification less than seven seconds per configuration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The entire pipeline is implemented as a reproducible Python script, available together with all input data in the accompanying repository.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/spiralulam/heilbronn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/spiralulam/heilbronn</a></span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Illustration of the methodology for <math id="S5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now illustrate the two-step pipeline on the case <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>, for which
Comellas and Yebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> heuristically obtained conjectured optimal
coordinates via simulated annealing followed by a local optimality analysis.
Global optimality was subsequently proved by Zeng and Chen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, who decomposed the problem into 226 nonlinear optimization subproblems.
Our computation independently confirms their result numerically and yields simpler coordinate expressions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Step 1: Numerical global optimization</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Running our final MINLP model from Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The final model ‣ 3 Mixed-integer nonlinear optimization models ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> with
<math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>, Gurobi terminates after 0.8 seconds (seed 1) with an objective value of <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0838594" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0838594</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0838594</annotation></semantics></math>, close to <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{7}\approx 0.0838590" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0838590</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{7}\approx 0.0838590</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the numerical configuration shown in
Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Step 1: Numerical global optimization ‣ 5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.fig3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:158.7pt;">
<table id="S5.T6.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T6.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pt</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T6.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math></th>
