<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Table 6: </span>Numerical coordinates returned by Gurobi for <math id="S5.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>
(rounded to four decimal places). Five of the seven points lie on the
boundary of the unit square.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11107v3/fig_n7.svg" id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/200;" width="238" height="200"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Optimal configuration for <math id="S5.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>
with its eight critical triangles highlighted.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Step 1: Numerical global optimization ‣ 5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the configuration
together with its eight critical triangles, which all have areas within
<math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> of each other. The ninth-smallest
triangle area is roughly <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0948" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0948</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0948</annotation></semantics></math>, leaving a comfortable gap
of about <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13\%</annotation></semantics></math> above the optimum.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Two structural observations guide the symbolic step that follows:</p>
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Five of the seven points lie on the boundary
(<math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{4},p_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{4},p_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> at corners, <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on the left edge,
<math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on the bottom edge, <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> on the right edge).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Points <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate,
suggesting a hidden algebraic relation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Step 2: Exact analytical refinement</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Guided by these observations, we set up a parametric ansatz.
The boundary structure fixes seven coordinates
(<math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{3}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{3}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{4}=y_{4}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{4}=y_{4}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{5}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{5}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{5}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{5}=1</annotation></semantics></math>),
and the shared <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate of <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces an additional coupling,
leaving six unknowns <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c,d,e,f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c,d,e,f</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Step 2: Exact analytical refinement ‣ 5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
