<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Parametric ansatz for <math id="S5.T7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>. Boundary constraints
fix seven coordinates, and the shared <math id="S5.T7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate of <math id="S5.T7.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.T7.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces the coupling <math id="S5.T7.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{7}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{7}=b</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Setting the areas of the eight critical triangles equal yields
seven polynomial equations in six unknowns.
A lexicographic Gröbner basis computation eliminates all unknowns
except <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, which must satisfy the irreducible cubic</p>
<table id="S5.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19\,f^{3}-27\,f^{2}+11\,f-1\;=\;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19\,f^{3}-27\,f^{2}+11\,f-1\;=\;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This cubic has three real roots:
<math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\approx 0.1269" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.1269</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\approx 0.1269</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}\approx 0.5839" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5839</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}\approx 0.5839</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{3}\approx 0.7103" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.7103</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{3}\approx 0.7103</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first root gives <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=19f_{1}^{2}-27f_{1}+10\approx 6.88&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.88</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=19f_{1}^{2}-27f_{1}+10\approx 6.88&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the third gives <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=19f_{3}^{2}-16f_{3}+3\approx 1.22&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=19f_{3}^{2}-16f_{3}+3\approx 1.22&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>;
both violate the unit-square constraint.
The only solution in the unit square therefore corresponds to <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Back-substitution through the Gröbner basis yields the
exact symbolic coordinates in Table <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Step 2: Exact analytical refinement ‣ 5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
