</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Exact symbolic coordinates of the optimal seven-point
configuration, where <math id="S5.T8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\approx 0.5839" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.5839</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\approx 0.5839</annotation></semantics></math> is the second real root of
<math id="S5.T8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19f^{3}-27f^{2}+11f-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19f^{3}-27f^{2}+11f-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The optimal value simplifies to</p>
<table id="S5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{7}\;=\;f-\tfrac{1}{2}\;\approx\;0.0838590090\ldots" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mn> 0.0838590090</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{7}\;=\;f-\tfrac{1}{2}\;\approx\;0.0838590090\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with Comellas and Yebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The exact coordinates we obtain are notably simpler than those
reported by Comellas and Yebra, who express
each coordinate as a degree-two polynomial in a parameter <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>
with rational coefficients over denominators <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>38</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38</annotation></semantics></math>
and integer numerators as large as <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="223" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>223</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">223</annotation></semantics></math>, for
instance <math id="S5.SS2.SSSx2.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}=-\tfrac{50}{19}\,z-\tfrac{17}{38}\,z^{2}+\tfrac{37}{38}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>50</mn><mn>19</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>17</mn><mn>38</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>37</mn><mn>38</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=-\tfrac{50}{19}\,z-\tfrac{17}{38}\,z^{2}+\tfrac{37}{38}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Exact configurations for <math id="S6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reports the exact configurations obtained with the framework of Section <a href="#S5" title="5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
Exact forms are also known for several best-known configurations with <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, which are not known to be optimal: for <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math> by Goldberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, for <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math> by Comellas and Yebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, for <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=14</annotation></semantics></math> by Chappell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and for <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> by Beyleveld (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Best-known configurations for 11≤𝑛≤16 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). No exact forms are known for the asymmetric best-known configurations for <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math>, due to Karpov, and <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math>, found by the author in 2026 with a heuristic search, which replaced Karpov’s configuration from 2011. The website of Tej Stead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> lists high-precision coordinates for all of them and exact values where known.
For every <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5,\dots,10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5,\dots,10</annotation></semantics></math>, we computed all <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> triangle areas of the configurations below in exact arithmetic over the respective number field. None of them is smaller than the stated value <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, and exactly the critical triangles attain it. For <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, these configurations are proven optimal, so <math id="S6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)=\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)=\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is a rigorous lower bound on <math id="S6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that matches the solver’s numerical upper bound. This verification takes less than seven seconds per configuration. For <math id="S6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, the members <math id="S6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1/8</annotation></semantics></math> of the one-parameter family were verified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> summarizes the values, Figure <a href="#S6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows the configurations with their critical triangles, and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Exact coordinates for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> lists the exact coordinates.
For <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial system defined by the critical triangles and the observed boundary structure has a unique solution in the unit square (this does not imply global uniqueness).
For <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, the configuration is determined by the equal-area conditions together with the stationarity condition of Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The framework ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.
For <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimal configurations form a one-parameter family with <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[0,\tfrac{1}{4}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[0,\tfrac{1}{4}]</annotation></semantics></math>, all with the same distribution of triangle areas, which is consistent with the count of active constraints in Section <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1 How many triangles are critical? ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>.
The derivation for <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> is given in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T9" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S6.T9.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T9.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T9.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S6.T9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S6.T9.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S6.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S6.T9.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S6.T9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)\approx</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S6.T9.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">critical triangles</th>
<th id="S6.T9.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">symmetry</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T9.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T9.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">5</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S6.T9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{3}/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{3}/9</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S6.T9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.19245" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.19245</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.19245</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">reflection in a diagonal</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T9.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T9.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/8</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.12500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.12500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.12500</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">rotation by <math id="S6.T9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="180^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> (family)</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T9.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T9.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">7</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f-\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f-\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>)</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.08386" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.08386</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.08386</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">8</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">none</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T9.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T9.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">8</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{13}-1)/36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{13}-1)/36</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.07238" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.07238</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.07238</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">12</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">rotation by <math id="S6.T9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="180^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T9.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T9.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">9</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(9\sqrt{65}-55)/320" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>65</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>55</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>320</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(9\sqrt{65}-55)/320</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.T9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05488" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05488</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05488</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">11</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">reflection in a diagonal</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T9.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T9.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">10</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S6.T9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{5}{8}g^{2}-\tfrac{1}{2}g^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{5}{8}g^{2}-\tfrac{1}{2}g^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Exact coordinates for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>)</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S6.T9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04654" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04654</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04654</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">16</td>
<td id="S6.T9.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">reflections in both diagonals</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
