</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Further observations and research questions</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section collects structural observations on the configurations for <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math> and on the best-known configurations for <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\leq n\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\leq n\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math> (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Best-known configurations for 11≤𝑛≤16 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).
For <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math> they are based on the exact coordinates of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. For <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math> they are based on coordinates with 30 significant digits, and two areas are considered equal if they differ by less than <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>How many triangles are critical?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At an optimal configuration, no small motion of the points can enlarge the smallest triangle: every such motion either shrinks a critical triangle or pushes a point out of the square. If the optimum is isolated and nondegenerate, this blocking requires at least as many active constraints as there are degrees of freedom, just as a vertex of a polyhedron in <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> lies on at least <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> facets.
To make this precise, note that locally, at a configuration without collinear points, the Heilbronn problem is the smooth problem of maximizing <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math> variables <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,z)</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leq\pm A^{\pm}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leq\pm A^{\pm}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for all triangles <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, with the sign of the current orientation, and to the box constraints.
At an optimal configuration, the active constraints are the critical triangles and the box constraints of the coordinates on <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial S</annotation></semantics></math>, where a corner contributes two. The box constraints cannot do much of the blocking:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmlemma4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At most eight box constraints are active at a configuration without three collinear points. Consequently, if at least <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math> constraints are active at an optimal configuration, then it has at least <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-7</annotation></semantics></math> critical triangles.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Every active box constraint corresponds to a pair of a point and an edge of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that contains it, where a corner belongs to two edges and accounts for two active constraints. No edge contains three points, since they would be collinear, so there are at most <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\cdot 2=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\cdot 2=8</annotation></semantics></math> such pairs. The second statement follows, since the critical triangles are the only other active constraints.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If optimal configurations are rigid in this sense, we therefore expect many critical triangles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para"><span id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<span id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An optimal configuration of <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> points has at least <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n-7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n-7</annotation></semantics></math> critical triangles.</span></span>
</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 7.1 How many triangles are critical? ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> tests Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1. ‣ 7.1 How many triangles are critical? ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> against all configurations of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Best-known configurations for 11≤𝑛≤16 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, and Table <a href="#S7.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ 7.1 How many triangles are critical? ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> lists the underlying counts.</p>
</div>
