<td id="S7.T10.2.4.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T10.2.4.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">13</span></td>
<td id="S7.T10.2.4.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T10.2.4.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3</span></td>
<td id="S7.T10.2.4.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T10.2.4.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0</span></td>
<td id="S7.T10.2.4.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T10.2.4.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">5</span></td>
<td id="S7.T10.2.4.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T10.2.4.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">0</span></td>
<td id="S7.T10.2.4.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T10.2.4.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">39</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Active constraints of the configurations of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (<math id="S7.T10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>) and of the best-known configurations (<math id="S7.T10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>). For <math id="S7.T10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, a member of the one-parameter family with <math id="S7.T10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;1/4</annotation></semantics></math> is used.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">All configurations satisfy the conjectured bound, with equality for <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of active constraints equals the number of variables exactly, as the rigidity heuristic predicts. The outlier <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> critical triangles, is explained by the special structure of that configuration (Section <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Clustering of noncritical triangle areas ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>).
The heuristic is not a proof, since a nonlinear optimum can be isolated with fewer active constraints than variables. The configuration for <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> is an example, with ten active constraints and eleven variables. It still satisfies the bound, as does the one-parameter family for <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, because fewer than eight box constraints are active in both cases. For <math id="S7.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, the missing active constraint matches the one degree of freedom of the family.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The conjecture is a lower bound on the number of active constraints. A remark of Oliver Stein points to an upper bound: if the linear independence constraint qualification (LICQ) holds, the gradients of the active constraints are linearly independent in <math id="S7.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so at most <math id="S7.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math> constraints are active. At a rigid optimum at which LICQ holds, both bounds meet, and exactly <math id="S7.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math> constraints are active. LICQ need not hold, however, and we checked it for all configurations of Table <a href="#S7.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ 7.1 How many triangles are critical? ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At the configurations of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, LICQ holds for <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, and it fails for <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math>, twelve triangle constraints are active by the exact verification (Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), and the corners <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and four further points on <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial S</annotation></semantics></math> give eight active box constraints. These <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> gradients in <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{17}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>17</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{17}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be linearly independent. For <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, sixteen triangle constraints and eight box constraints, one for each of the eight points on <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial S</annotation></semantics></math>, give <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math> gradients in <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>21</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{21}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the other values of <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we computed the rank of the matrix of active gradients exactly by Gaussian elimination over the respective number field. For <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>, the rank equals the number of active constraints (<math id="S7.SS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math>). For the member <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1/8</annotation></semantics></math> of the family for <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> active gradients have rank <math id="S7.SS1.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The configurations for <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> are therefore nondegenerate in the strongest sense: exactly <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math> constraints are active, and their gradients are linearly independent.
For <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, LICQ fails although fewer constraints than variables are active, which reflects the one-parameter family of optima.
For the best-known configurations, LICQ fails whenever more than <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n+1</annotation></semantics></math> constraints are active, that is, for <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math>, a numerical rank computation with 50 digits indicates that it holds, with smallest singular values <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.045</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.045</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.022" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.022</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.022</annotation></semantics></math> of the matrix of active gradients.
The script for these computations is part of the accompanying repository.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Clustering of noncritical triangle areas</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For many configurations, including those of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the areas of the noncritical triangles do not spread out but cluster at a small number of values, and all areas in a cluster are equal.
Figure <a href="#S7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 7.2 Clustering of noncritical triangle areas ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>a shows this for <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>: after the four critical triangles, the remaining six areas take only three values.
Figure <a href="#S7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 7.2 Clustering of noncritical triangle areas ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>b shows the number of distinct noncritical areas relative to the number of noncritical triangles for <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5,\dots,16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5,\dots,16</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S7.F9.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:151.8pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.11107v3/degen_plot_n5.svg" id="S7.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:210/94;" width="210" height="94"></object>
