<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>(a) Multiplicities of the distinct triangle areas of the optimal configuration for <math id="S7.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>: the minimum <math id="S7.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{3}/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{3}/9</annotation></semantics></math> is attained by four triangles, and the remaining six areas form three clusters of sizes <math id="S7.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.F9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.F9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) Number of distinct noncritical triangle areas divided by the number of noncritical triangles, for the configurations of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Exact configurations for 5≤𝑛≤10 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (<math id="S7.F9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math>) and the best-known configurations (<math id="S7.F9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>). Small values indicate strong clustering.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Symmetry explains part of the clustering, since a symmetry of the configuration maps triangles to triangles of the same area, but not all of it.
The configuration for <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> has no symmetry, yet its <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>27</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27</annotation></semantics></math> noncritical triangles take only <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>13</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13</annotation></semantics></math> distinct values, and one value is attained by five triangles, namely <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2,7)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,5,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,5,7)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3,7)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3,5)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,5,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,5,7)</annotation></semantics></math> in the labeling of Table <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Step 2: Exact analytical refinement ‣ 5.2 Illustration of the methodology for 𝑛=7 ‣ 5 The optimize-then-refine framework ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
For two of them there is a simple reason: <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> share their <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coordinate, so <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2,7)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,5,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,5,7)</annotation></semantics></math> have the common base <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}p_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}p_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> and their apex on the left edge. We have no comparably simple explanation for the other three.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The configuration for <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> shows a different source of clustering. As observed by Tej Stead (personal communication, 2026), Beyleveld’s configuration is an affine image of the vertex set of the four-dimensional cube: it consists of the points</p>
<table id="S7.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\tfrac{2}{31},0\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{4}\varepsilon_{i}e_{i},\qquad\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}^{4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>31</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\tfrac{2}{31},0\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{4}\varepsilon_{i}e_{i},\qquad\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}^{4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}=\left(\tfrac{21}{31},0\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>21</mn><mn>31</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=\left(\tfrac{21}{31},0\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}=\left(-\tfrac{2}{31},\tfrac{10}{33}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>31</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>33</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}=\left(-\tfrac{2}{31},\tfrac{10}{33}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{3}=\left(\tfrac{8}{31},\tfrac{2}{33}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>31</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>33</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{3}=\left(\tfrac{8}{31},\tfrac{2}{33}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{4}=\left(0,\tfrac{7}{11}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>11</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{4}=\left(0,\tfrac{7}{11}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Every triangle area of such a point set is an integer combination of the six determinants <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det(e_{i},e_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det(e_{i},e_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, all <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="560" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>560</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">560</annotation></semantics></math> areas are integer multiples of <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7/682" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>682</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7/682</annotation></semantics></math>. They take only <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>31</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31</annotation></semantics></math> distinct values, and the minimum <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7/341" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>341</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7/341</annotation></semantics></math> is attained by <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math> triangles.
This structure does not generalize in an obvious way: the optimal configuration for <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> is not an affine image of the vertex set of the three-dimensional cube.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Not all configurations cluster, however. Almost all triangle areas of the asymmetric best-known configurations for <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct (Figure <a href="#S7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 7.2 Clustering of noncritical triangle areas ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>b). Either these configurations are not optimal, or the clustering is limited to small <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and to configurations with special structure. This leads to the following question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para"><span id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="Thmopenquestion1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_openquestion">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmopenquestion1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open question 1</span></span><span id="Thmopenquestion1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<span id="Thmopenquestion1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="Thmopenquestion1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Do the noncritical triangle areas of optimal Heilbronn configurations generally cluster at only a few values, and if so, why?</span>
</span>
</span></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Outlook and final remarks</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have presented an <em id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimize-then-refine</em> framework for the Heilbronn triangle problem on the unit square. An MINLP model whose structure rests on Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Structure of optimal configurations). ‣ 2.2 Boundary structure of optimal configurations ‣ 2 Original formulation and theoretical results ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> solves all <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 10</annotation></semantics></math> to global optimality within solver tolerances without precomputation, and exact symbolic computation turns the numerical solutions into exactly verified configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several directions remain open.
First, the upper bounds of this paper are numerical. A rigorous proof of optimality for <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> would require the dual bounds to be validated, for example by interval-based global optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, by verified certificates for the branch-and-bound tree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, or by an independent verification of the kind used for the traveling salesman problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
Second, the next cases are within reach. The step from <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=9</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> multiplied the running time by about 36, and a similar factor would put <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math> at about a week on a laptop. Since the growth comes from the dual bound, stronger relaxations or valid inequalities are the natural next step.
Third, Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1. ‣ 7.1 How many triangles are critical? ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on critical triangles and Open question <a href="#Thmopenquestion1" title="Open question 1. ‣ 7.2 Clustering of noncritical triangle areas ‣ 7 Further observations and research questions ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on the clustering of triangle areas remain open. The latter is directly relevant for the search for better configurations for <math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More broadly, the two-step methodology applies to other packing and covering problems in discrete geometry whose conjectured optimal configurations involve algebraic numbers of moderate degree.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank the two anonymous referees and the technical editor for their careful reading and their constructive comments, which improved the paper considerably.
We thank Tej Stead for maintaining the record tables of the Heilbronn problem and for pointing out the cube structure of the configuration for <math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math>,
Erich Friedman for kindly sharing the previously unpublished best-known configurations of Mark Beyleveld (<math id="Sx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=14,16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=14,16</annotation></semantics></math>),
Rhys Chappell for the exact form of the configuration for <math id="Sx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=14</annotation></semantics></math>,
Amirali Modir for the connection between minimal covering parallelograms and the boundary structure,
and Oliver Stein for his remark on constraint qualifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
