<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 13: </span>Exact coordinates for <math id="A1.T13.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.T13.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\approx 0.3156" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.3156</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\approx 0.3156</annotation></semantics></math> is the real root of <math id="A1.T13.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12g^{3}-27g^{2}+20g-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12g^{3}-27g^{2}+20g-4</annotation></semantics></math>, explicitly <math id="A1.T13.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\frac{3}{4}-\frac{(63+8\sqrt{62})^{1/3}}{12}-\frac{1}{12(63+8\sqrt{62})^{1/3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>62</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>62</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\frac{3}{4}-\frac{(63+8\sqrt{62})^{1/3}}{12}-\frac{1}{12(63+8\sqrt{62})^{1/3}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The minimum area is <math id="A1.T13.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)=\frac{5}{8}g^{2}-\frac{1}{2}g^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)=\frac{5}{8}g^{2}-\frac{1}{2}g^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Best-known configurations for <math id="A2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\leq n\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\leq n\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For completeness, we collect the best-known configurations for <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\leq n\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\leq n\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math> as of October 2026. Records for larger <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> are maintained on the website of Tej Stead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, and coordinates with 30 significant digits for all configurations below are available in the accompanying repository.
The configuration for <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math> is due to Comellas and Yebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>,
the one for <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=11</annotation></semantics></math> to Goldberg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, the one for <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math> to Peter Karpov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, the ones for <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=14</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> to Mark Beyleveld, and the one for <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> to the author (found in 2026 with a heuristic search).
For <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=13</annotation></semantics></math> we list Karpov’s configuration with fully converged coordinates as given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. The stated lower bounds refer to the full-precision coordinates. For <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=14</annotation></semantics></math>, Rhys Chappell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> found an exact form: <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>14</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{14}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded below by the smallest positive root of <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="320x^{3}+768x^{2}-60x+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>320</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>768</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">320x^{3}+768x^{2}-60x+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The exact coordinates are listed in
Tables <a href="#A2.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Appendix B Best-known configurations for 11≤𝑛≤16 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ Appendix B Best-known configurations for 11≤𝑛≤16 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, and <a href="#A2.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ Appendix B Best-known configurations for 11≤𝑛≤16 ‣ From Global Optimization to Exact Coordinates:Heilbronn’s Triangle Problem on the Unit SquareUsing Mixed-Integer Nonlinear Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.
In the entry for <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=1-\frac{(27+3\sqrt{57})^{2/3}+6}{6(27+3\sqrt{57})^{1/3}}\approx 0.1154" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>57</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>57</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.1154</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=1-\frac{(27+3\sqrt{57})^{2/3}+6}{6(27+3\sqrt{57})^{1/3}}\approx 0.1154</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=2x^{2}-3x+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=2x^{2}-3x+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T14" class="ltx_table">
